{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Prohibition on rent increases above rent control or unconscionable increases","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent above any applicable rent control or rent leveling ordinance, and for housing not subject to rent control may not impose an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental dwellings; rent-controlled units and units not subject to rent control.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"An increase in rent in excess of any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or if the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.4,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act grounds for criminal conviction offenses","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of, or found delinquent for, assault or terrorist threats against the landlord, the landlord's family or employees, theft of property, or human trafficking; a three-day Notice to Quit is required.","key_value":"3-day Notice to Quit; suit barred 2 years after conviction or release","coverage_conditions":"Tenants covered by the Anti-Eviction Act, including a tenant who knowingly permits a person convicted of these offenses to reside on the premises continuously or occasionally.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No eviction suit may be brought more than two years after: the juvenile was found to be delinquent; conviction of the person; or after the person’s release from incarceration, whichever is later. A Notice to Quit must be served on the tenant at least three day s prior to filing a suit for (N.J.S.A. 2A-18-61.1(o)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction from owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","requirement":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings may be evicted only by court order, and only for holding over, nonpayment, disorderly conduct, destruction of the premises, or violation of written rules or lease provisions.","key_value":"3-month notice (at will or year-to-year); 1-month (month-to-month); 3-day (disorderly, destructive or rule violations)","coverage_conditions":"Landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings; eviction only when a court issues an order.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction. However, in these cases, the landlord must prove that the tenant (a) is staying after the expiration of the term of the lease, (b) is staying after a failure to pay rent,","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house eviction good cause and notice rules","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good-cause and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, and must give at least three working days' notice to the County Welfare Board before starting an eviction action.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Rooming and boarding houses: buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"rooming and boarding houses"},"exemptions":"Rooming and Boarding House Act exceptions: hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for eviction law violations after owner-occupancy or retirement notices","requirement":"After a tenant vacates on a notice of personal occupancy, the landlord must occupy the unit at least six months; after a notice of permanent retirement from residential use, the unit may not be used residentially for five years.","key_value":"6 months' owner occupancy; 5-year residential-use ban","coverage_conditions":"Dwelling units vacated after notice that the landlord will personally occupy the unit, or that the building will be permanently boarded up, demolished or retired from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs; for the five-year residential-use ban also a civil penalty up to $10,000.00 per violation.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"When a tenant vacates a dwelling unit after having been given notice that the landlord wishes to personally occupy the unit the landlord must occupy the unit for at least six (6) months. If instead the landlord permits personal occupancy of the unit by another tenant or registration of conversion of the property to a condominium or cooperative, the landlord is liable to the former tenant for three (3) times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6(a)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Reprisal ban on eviction for exercising tenant civil rights","requirement":"A landlord may not evict, substantially alter the lease, or refuse to renew in reprisal for a tenant's exercising protected civil rights, such as a good faith complaint about health or safety violations.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":null},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"The tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Foreclosure eviction notice protections for tenants","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction; a buyer who wants to occupy the foreclosed unit as a primary residence must give the tenant a 90-day notice to quit, and the new owner must notify tenants within 10 business days after the sale.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit; 10 business days to notify tenants","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants in foreclosed properties; month-to-month tenants and owner-occupied two- and three-family units are not exempt from the 90-day notice.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69","quoted_span":"Before a tenant can be evicted due to foreclosure, the landlord must provide the tenant with a 90 -day notice to qui t when the foreclosed property has been purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as his or her primary residence.","interaction":"Federal law does not preempt any State or local law that provides longer time periods or other additional protections for tenants in foreclosure proceedings.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act eviction protection","requirement":"Qualified senior citizens, permanently disabled tenants and certain disabled veterans who lived in a converting building at least one year before conversion may be protected from eviction for 40 years if family income does not exceed three times county average per-person income or $50,000, whichever is greater.","key_value":"40-year protection; income limit 3x county average per-person income or $50,000.00, whichever is greater","coverage_conditions":"Tenants at least 62 before the conversion recording, permanently disabled tenants, or veterans disabled 60% or more from service, who lived in the building at least one year before the conversion recording date.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"condominium, cooperative or fee simple conversion"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever  is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 protected tenancy in qualified counties","requirement":"The 1992 Tenant Protection Law extends eviction protections during conversion to qualified tenants in qualified counties who were not eligible as senior citizens or disabled persons under the 1981 Act.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in buildings converted or being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership; currently only Hudson County is a qualified county.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Hudson County conversions"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4). At the present time, the only qualified county is Hudson County (N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.2(b)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
